      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's themes of technological anxiety and body transformation align with contemporaneous Japanese cyberpunk works like <a href=\"/movie/149\" class=\"movie-title\">Akira</a> (1988) and later influenced Western sci-fi horror like <a href=\"/movie/11309\" class=\"movie-title\">Hardware</a> (1990). Its surreal narrative structure and industrial soundtrack by Chu Ishikawa created a template for experimental tech-horror that echoes through films like <a href=\"/movie/48191\" class=\"movie-title\">Rubber's Lover</a> (1996) and <a href=\"/movie/27351\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"27351\">Meatball Machine</a> (2005)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Tsukamoto's guerrilla filmmaking approach, using stop-motion animation and practical effects, demonstrates how low-budget innovation can create visceral impact. This DIY aesthetic influenced later digital-age body horror like Kairo (2001) and <a href=\"/movie/11366\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"11366\">Uzumaki</a> (2000), while its themes of technological infection presaged modern techno-anxiety films like <a href=\"/movie/435615\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"435615\">Possessor</a> (2020). SUBHEAD: Impact on Modern Body Horror"
      },
      {
